{"v":1,"accession":"PRJNA1465925","title":"Integration of traditional modern and environmental DNA methods to assess mammalian diversity in high-biodiversity areas","description":"The Amazon is one of the planets major biodiversity hotspots; however, the composition of mammal communities remains limited. This article assesses the diversity and community structure of mammals in the buffer zone of the Cordillera Escalera Regional Conservation Area (CE-ACR), San Martin Department, Peru, using complementary methods to record different groups of mammals: mist nets and ultrasonic detectors for small flying mammals, Sherman traps, Tomahawk, and impact traps for small non-flying mammals, and camera traps for medium and large mammals, with a total sampling effort of 240 net-nights, 288 hours of acoustic monitoring, 4800 trap-nights, and 2171 camera-days. Additionally, air-samplers for environmental DNA were used. Sampling completeness was assessed using rarefaction curves and coverage-based extrapolation. We recorded 87 mammal species: 57 bats, 9 small non-flying mammals, and 21 medium and large mammals. Bats represented the most diverse group, with a predominance of the family Phyllostomidae. The rarefaction curves indicated high sampling completeness in all three mammal groups (greater than 97 percent). Here we demonstrate the high biological value of the buffer zone of the CE-ACR and highlight the importance of private conservation properties for the protection of Amazonian fauna. The combination of sampling methods allowed a comprehensive assessment of mammalian diversity.","center":"Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","submission_date":"2026/05/13 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SA